\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Source horizon and selection hypothesis}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}
\tag{harness}

\p{The direct observation in
\citet{Abstract; Sections 3-5 and Tables 1-4}{dang2026selecting} compares
4K, 8K, 12K, and 16K supervision horizons for terminal-agent SFT. In its
reported Terminal-Bench v1.0 setting, 12K reaches more tasks than 16K with
about thirty percent less training time. The shorter setting under-persisted,
the longest setting over-persisted, and the intermediate setting produced the
best reported balance. This is a finite terminal-agent observation under one
model, teacher corpus, scaffold, and benchmark family; it does not establish a
universal horizon, long-term memory, or persistent identity.}

\p{The paper's bias-complexity account gives the FTIP hypothesis
\citet{Sections 5.2 and 6-7; Tables 4-5, 10}{dang2026selecting}. Truncation
can increase temporal-supervision bias by removing later decisions, while a
longer continuation increases the policy complexity and finite-sample burden.
The useful horizon is therefore conditional on the task law, teacher policy,
model capacity, verifier, and retained state.}

\p{Selective refinement makes the data intervention explicit
\citet{Sections 6-7; Tables 4-5, 10}{dang2026selecting}. A warm-start model is
trained on a shorter horizon, continuation segments are scored by
teacher-forced likelihood, and a fixed fraction is retained for longer-horizon
refinement. The score measures compatibility with the warm-start policy, not
intrinsic truth. High-likelihood selection can improve repeatability; the FTIP
protocol tests whether low-likelihood or random selection preserves breadth,
rare strategies, and recovery behavior.}

\p{A run is \strong{premature} when it terminates before the declared evidence
or recovery condition, \strong{productive} when it continues through bounded
evidence gathering, verification, and recovery, and \strong{over-persistent}
when additional work repeats or explores after task-relevant value has
saturated. Record coverage loss separately: a selector can improve
repeatability while deleting hard tasks or unusual recovery paths. These terms
describe observable behavior; they are not claims about an agent's memory or
inner objective.}
